# Defect‐Dependent Crystal Plane Control on Inorganic CsPbBr3 Film by Selectively Anchoring (Pseudo‐) Halide Anions for 1.650 V Voltage Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
One of the key problems to improving the performance of a typical perovskite solar cell (PSC) is to minimize the defect‐determined nonradiative recombination. So far, the cutting‐edge strategies mainly focus on the film‐scale defect manipulation at grain boundaries and surfaces by trial‐and‐error, but the intrinsic passivation mechanism and fundamental guideline in the perspective of crystal lattices are still unclear. Herein, a much lower defect formation energy of PbBr antisite defect at (110) plane than that at (h00) planes is demonstrated by theoretically studying the crystal plane‐dependent defect density in an inorganic CsPbBr3 film, and then the authors precisely control the plane growth by selectively anchoring (pseudo‐) halide anions from ionic liquids to (110) plane. Because of the different adsorption energies on various planes, the growth of defective (110) plane is suppressed, leading to the formation of (h00) plane‐oriented film. Finally, the all‐inorganic CsPbBr3 PSC passivated by ionic liquid EMImCl delivers the best efficiency of 10.71% with an open‐circuit voltage up to 1.650 V. This work provides an in‐depth insight into defect formation and passivation mechanism to stabilize perovskite photovoltaics.

One of the key problems to improving the performance of a typical perovskite solar cell (PSC) is to minimize the defect-determined nonradiative recombination. So far, the cutting-edge strategies mainly focus on the film-scale defect manipulation at grain boundaries and surfaces by trial-and-error, but the intrinsic passivation mechanism and fundamental guideline in the perspective of crystal lattices are still unclear. Herein, a much lower defect formation energy of $\mathsf{P b}_{\mathsf{B r}}$ antisite defect at (110) plane than that at (h00) planes is demonstrated by theoretically studying the crystal plane-dependent defect density in an inorganic $\mathsf{c s P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3}$ film, and then the authors precisely control the plane growth by selectively anchoring (pseudo-) halide anions from ionic liquids to (110) plane. Because of the different adsorption energies on various planes, the growth of defective (110) plane is suppressed, leading to the formation of (h00) plane-oriented film. Finally, the all-inorganic $\mathsf{c s P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3}$ PSC passivated by ionic liquid EMImCl delivers the best efficiency of $10.71\%$ with an open-circuit voltage up to 1.650 V. This work provides an in-depth insight into defect formation and passivation mechanism to stabilize perovskite photovoltaics.
## 1. Introduction
Given the rapid power conversion efficiency (PCE) improvement from $3.8\%$ to certified $25.7\%$ , perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have made a great step toward commercialization.[1–4] Reviewing the development of PSC in the past decade, healing detrimental defects formed during the rapid nucleation and crystallization from precursor solution shows an indelible contribution to suppress charge recombination and to increase the performance, which is regarded as an origin for soft perovskite lattice decomposition under humidity, oxygen, heat, light, and electric field.[5–11] Therefore, the stateof-the-art studies are mainly focused on the film-dimensional manipulation through crystallization control, surfaces and grain boundaries passivation or mechanical polishing treatment,[14–18] comprehensively clarifying the fundamental understanding on defect species and the interaction mechanism with additives (such as Lewis acid-base chemistry). However, what is the relation from defective grain surface to lattice plane for guiding target passivators design?
In general, high-quality perovskite monocrystalline or polycrystalline films with monolayered grains are desired to minimize the grain boundaries and defect densities — or rather, exposed crystal planes;[19,20] that is, defective crystal planes basically determine the overall charge trapping centers in perovskite films. The similar phenomena have been empirically reported, such as the anisotropic electronic properties of orientated film,[21] and that carrier lifetime, open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ loss, and hysteresis highly correlate with the {111} and {110} twin domains,[22,23] demonstrating the non-negligible effect of in-plane feature on optoelectronic performances. In this fashion, the precise control on preferable crystal plane growth is expected to maximize charge transfer and power output.
In comparison to other perovskite halides, inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ presents an excellent tolerance to harsh environment and a large bandgap of ${\approx}2.3\mathrm{eV},$ therefore it shows great potential in tandem and semi-transparent solar cells.[24–26] But the large optical bandgap in turn enlarges the probability to form deep traps within bandgap, particularly Pb-Br $(\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{Br}})$ antisite defects.[27] As a result, the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss of inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ PSC is much more notorious than that of narrow-bandgap perovskite photovoltaics.[28] In details, the minimal $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSCs is $0.598\mathrm{~V},$ which is much higher than $0.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs, $0.34\mathrm{~V~}$ for $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs, $0.44\mathrm{~V~}$ for $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.17}\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.4}\mathrm{Br}_{0.6})_{3}$ PSCs, $0.38\mathrm{~V~}$ for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ PSCs and other lower bandgap PSCs.[29–37] Considering that the serious $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss is highly related to the defects in perovskite film, it is understandable that there are more defects in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film than that of other films. Therefore, it is advantageous to reveal the relationship between defect and crystal plane by studying $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ solar cell. To reveal the plane-dependent defect distribution, in this work, we have theoretically calculated the defect formation energies on exposed planes of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite lattice, demonstrating the largest defect formation energy in (h00) planes. Guided by the theoretical calculation, we have further precisely controlled the crystal plane growth by anchoring several (pseudo-) halide anions (including ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ ) with various adsorption energies on the defective planes. As a result, high-quality $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film with markedly reduced defect density because of the orientation of benign (h00) plane and elimination of mobile (110) plane is obtained, finally forwarding the PCE of all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSC to $10.71\%$ with an ultrahigh $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.650\mathrm{V}.$ .
## 2. Results and Discussion
Generally, there are several characteristic diffraction peaks in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film even without controlling grain growth, indicating the nonisotropic crystal planes.[29,38,39] To study the intrinsic mechanism, we first calculated the surface energies of characteristic (100), (110), (111), (200), (210), and (211) planes to be -142.32, -140.35, -138.72, -142.32, -139.84, and $-136.63\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ (Figure S1, Supporting Information), respectively. The surface energies of (100), (200) and (110) planes are much lower than those for other planes, which indicates the growth propensity of perovskite grain primary along [h00] direction and subordinated [110] direction.[40] Therefore, we here investigated the formation energies of $\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{Br}}$ defects on (100), (110), and (200) surfaces by density functional theory (DFT) calculation to in-depth understand the defective planes. According to the projected density of states (PDOS) of three $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ slabs (Figure 1a–c), (h00) planes show the defect-free feature, and (110) plane suffers from evident trap states. The formation energies of $\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{Br}}$ defects are determined to be $-0.60$ and $-1.15\mathrm{eV}$ on (h00) and (110) planes, respectively. Therefore, the film-scale defects mainly originate from the exposed (110) plane regardless of another defective (211) plane (Figure S2, Supporting Information) because of the largest surface energy (the smallest amount in final film), which is doubtless a malignant tumor accounting for the charge recombination and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss.
Considering ionic liquids have been widely used to passivate perovskites because of their versatile and unique properties including electrochemical and thermal stability, high ionic conductivity, and superior solubility,[41–46] we employed a series of popularly-used ionic liquids sharing identical 1-ethyl3-methylimidazolium (EMIm) cations but with strategically varying anions (including ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ ) as additives to regulate $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film crystallization and to study the plane-dependent interactions. As evidenced by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in Figure 1d, the incorporation of ionic liquid does not change the crystal phase with characteristic diffraction peaks centered at $15.4^{\circ}$ , $21.8^{\circ}$ , and $30.8^{\circ}$ for (100), (110) and (200) crystal planes, respectively. But there is an obvious deviation on the peak intensity, particularly the suppression of (110) peak together with the enhanced (h00) peak, which is visible from the ratio of (h00)/(110) peaks (Figure  1e). This undoubtedly indicates that ionic liquids control the growth of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ crystal along [h00] direction.[47] In addition, the orientation degree highly depends on the anion species by obeying an order of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}\mathrm{\Delta>\BF_{4}^{\mathrm{-}}>\ P F_{6}^{\mathrm{-}}>\ N O_{3}^{\mathrm{-}}}}$ . Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) images of perovskite films in Figure  1g,h shows that ionic liquids tailored perovskite films exhibit gradually increased surface potential by following the same order of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}>B F_{4}^{-}>P F_{6}^{-}>N O_{3}^{-},}$ along with the narrowed potential distribution from average 37.5 to $33.2\mathrm{mV}.$ This is attributed to the reduction of defective (110) plane and enrichment of flawless (h00) plane on the film surface, as illustrated in Figure 1f. Consequently, the trap density is significantly reduced from $3.63\times\mathrm{\bar{10}^{15}\ c m^{-3}}$ to $2.16\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and the electron mobility is enhanced by recording the dark current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves based on the space-charge-limited current (SCLC) model (Figure S3 and Table S1, Supporting Information),[48] although the surface morphology is nearly unchanged from scanning electron microscope (SEM) and atomic force microscope (AFM) images in Figure S4, Supporting Information. The enhanced surface photoluminescence (PL) of perovskite films upon treatment from the confocal PL mapping images (Figure  1i, Figure S5, and Figure S6, Supporting Information) further ensures the elimination of exposed defects, which agrees well with the abovementioned characterizations.
It is well known that organic cations in ionic liquid can interact with the perovskite lattice to passivate the ionic defects in the perovskite films and increase the hydrophobic ability of perovskite film.[44–46,49] As shown in Figure S7, Supporting Information, the shift of $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretching vibration peak of $\mathrm{EMIm^{+}}$ toward the lower wavenumber after incorporation with $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ and CsBr suggests a weakened bond, demonstrating the interaction between $\mathrm{EMIm^{+}}$ and the under-coordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cs^{+}}}$ species in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ lattice. However, it should be noted that the cations in four additives are the same, therefore, the anions play a more important role during crystallization. Considering that the energetics of crystal plane determines the corresponding growth dynamics, the absorption energies $(E_{\mathrm{ads}})$ of anions on various planes have thereby been calculated to better understand the intrinsic mechanism behind the crystal orientation growth. Figure 2 shows the molecular structures of four ionic liquids and sketches of the interactions between anions and crystal planes of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ . Compared to the (100) and (200) planes, all the anions deliver the smallest $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ on (110) plane with 0.46, 0.99, 1.06, and $1.38\:\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{PF}}_{6}{}^{-}$ , and ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , respectively. In other words, these anions preferably anchor onto the (110) surface rather than (h00) surface during the grain growth.[50] This hinders the former epitaxial growth and benefits the oriented growth along [h00] direction, which in turn reduces the overall defect density of final perovskite film.
Through further parallel comparison, it is readily understood that the orientation degree follows the order of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}>B F_{4}^{-}>}$ $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}>\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ in respect of $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ evolution. In detail, $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ can strongly interact with (110) plane and even enter the superficial lattice to form $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{Pb\mathrm{-}C l}}$ or $\mathrm{Cs-Cl}$ bond to diminish the $\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{Br}}$ antisite defects (slight shift of characteristic peak in XRD pattern is observed in Figure S8, Supporting Information), which is cross-checked by the shift of Pb 4f and Cs 3d core-level X-ray photo electron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra to higher binding energies (Figure S9, Supporting Information).[27,51] Notably, the emphasis is mainly focused on the interaction between anions and crystal planes without considering the cation species. Therefore, other materials (not limited to ionic liquids) with the same anions are expected to display similar role in determining the perovskite quality, demonstrating that the selective anchoring of (pseudo-) halide anions is of great concern in chemistry to improve film quality and passivate in-planar defects.
To study the impact of oriented perovskite film on photovoltaic performances, we subsequently fabricated an all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSC with a configuration of fluorine-doped tin dioxide $\mathrm{(FTO)}/c\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/m\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}$ carbon. As shown in Figure  3a, the statistical photovoltaic data including PCE, $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ , and fill factor (FF) of ionic liquids tailored PSCs are much higher than those for the control device by incorporating anions into perovskites. Figure 3b shows the characteristic $J{-}V$ curves of champion PSCs and the detailed photovoltaic data are summarized in Table S2, Supporting Information. The EMImCl tailored device delivers a champion PCE as high as $10.71\%$ , which is one of the highest records for this kind of PSCs (Table S3, Supporting Information). Notably, EMImCl tailored $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSC yields an impressive $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.650~\mathrm{V},$ which is much higher than $1.563\mathrm{~V~}$ for the control PSC, suggesting the significantly reduced $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss from 0.737 to $0.650~\mathrm{V}.$ Figure 3c presents the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of control and optimized PSCs with integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values of 6.81, 7.21, 7.45, 7.62, and $7.74\mathrm{\mAcm^{-2}}$ for control, ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{PF}}_{6}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ tailored devices, respectively, which are in good agreement with the data from $J{-}V$ curves and the increased light absorption ability (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Moreover, compared to the control PSCs, the optimized PSC displays the significantly reduced hysteresis owing to the oriented perovskite film with reduced defects (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Together with the matched steady PCEs under maximum power points (MPPs) for all devices, as shown in Figure 3d, we can confirm the positive effect of ionic liquids on cell performance improvement due to the increased Voc and Jsc.
In a PSC device, the defects determine the photogenerated carrier transfer and recombination behaviors. To gain more insights into this electron-defect coupling process, we measured the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ as a function of light intensity (I). As shown in Figure S12, Supporting Information, there is a linear relationship between $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and light intensity, in which the slightly increased coefficient from 0.953 to 0.998 indicates the improved charge transfer and reduced bimolecular recombination owing to the matchable energy alignment (Figure S13, Supporting Information).[52] By contrast, the slope derived from the relationship between $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and the logarithm of light intensity is significantly reduced from 1.911 $k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q$ for the control device to $1.620~k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q$ for champion device (Figure  3e), where $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is temperature and $\boldsymbol{q}$ is the elementary charge, evidently demonstrating the suppressed trap-assisted non-radiative recombination.[53] The elimination of defective (110) plane is undoubtedly regarded as the dominated origin for the reduced recombination. This agrees well with the prolonged carrier lifetime from time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decay spectra (Figure S14 and Table S4, Supporting Information) and the enlarged recombination resistance from electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurement (Figure S15 and Table S5, Supporting Information). Moreover, the increased built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ extracted from the capacitance–voltage $(C-V)$ curves (Figure  3f) means an enhanced driving force to cause a much lower dark current density with one order of magnitude for champion device than that of the control device (Figure 3g), resulting in the intensification in $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ .[54]
Finally, we further investigated the long-term durability of control and oriented $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSCs free of encapsulation against harsh environment stimuli. As shown in Figure 4a and Figure S16, Supporting Information, the best PSC remains over $96\%$ of its initial efficiency, whereas the control device drops to $88\%$ after storage over 30 days under air atmosphere with $85\%$ relative humidity (RH) and $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Compared to other PSCs based on hybrid or I-containing perovskite halides, the superior environmental tolerance of all-inorganic $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\mathrm{PSC}$ undoubtedly promotes the practical applications.[55,56] More importantly, the incorporation of EMImCl also significantly improves the operational stability by persistently irradiating the device under one standard sun over $500\mathrm{{h}}$ (Figure 4b). Apart from the organic cation-induced hydrophobic surface (enlarged contact angle in Figure S17, Supporting Information), the reduction of defective (110) plane and enhancement of $(\mathrm{h}00)$ plane is responsible for the prolonged shelf-life of PSC because the (h00) plane is more stable than other planes by taking the smallest free energy of (h00) plane into consideration.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have systematically investigated the dependence of defects on crystal plane in inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite, the theoretical calculation demonstrates that the (h00) planes are immaculate and advantageous for photovoltaic performances. A series of ionic liquids with various anions are used as additives to regulate the crystal growth dynamics. Arising from the lowest absorption energy on the (110) plane for each anion, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film is preferable to grow along [h00] direction to eliminate defective (110) plane and to reduce defect density in perovskite film. Upon assembling into solar cells, EMImCl tailored all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSC delivers a champion efficiency as high as $10.71\%$ with an impressive $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.650~\mathrm{V},$ which is much higher than $8.92\%$ for the control device. Our findings offer an in-depth understanding on the relationship between defects and crystal planes, which may be regarded as a universal guideline for choosing specific materials to control the film quality and promoting the future development of perovskite-based optoelectronic devices.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Unless indicated otherwise, all the materials and reagents were purchased from commercial suppliers and used without further purification. The ionic liquid additives $(\mathsf{E M I m N O}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{E M I m P F}_{6}$ , $\mathsf{E M I m B F}_{4}$ $\mathsf{E M l m C l}_{\prime}^{\prime}$ were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation.
Fabrication of PSCs: First, a designed pattern was etched on the FTO glass with zinc powders and hydrochloric acid, and then the above FTO glass was rinsed with deionized water, absolute ethanol, acetone, and isopropanol under ultrasonic cleaning. Next, an ethanol solution of $\mathsf{\Omega}_{0.5\mathsf{M}}$ diethanol amine and $0.5\mathsf{M}$ titanium isopropoxide was spin-coated on the FTO surface at $7000~\mathsf{r m p}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , and then sintered for $2h$ at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to obtain the compact titanium dioxide $(c\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2})$ layer. The mesoscopic $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ $(m{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2})$ film was deposited on the $\mathsf{F T O}/c\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}$ surface by spin-coating the as-prepared $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ paste at 2000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and annealed in muffle furnace at $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $0.5{\ h}$ . Finally, the $\mathsf{F T O}/c\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}/m\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}$ samples were immersed in a $40\mathrm{\mM\TiCl_{4}}$ aqueous solution for $0.5{\ h}$ under $75^{\circ}C$ , and then annealed at $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for another $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ perovskite films were fabricated by a multi-step solution deposition technology. First, 1  M $\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ in N,N-dimethylformamide solution containing $1\%$ molar ratios of ionic liquid additives $(\mathsf{E M I m N O}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{E M I m P F}_{6},$ $\mathsf{E M I m B F}_{4}$ $\mathsf{E M l m C l}$ ) was spin-coated onto the prepared $\mathsf{F T O}/c\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}/m\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}$ substrate at 2000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . It was then dried at $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $0.5{\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then, ${90\ \upmu\up L}$ of $0.07~\mathsf{M}$ CsBr methanol solution was spin-coated on $\mathsf{F T O}/c\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}/m\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ substrate at 2000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , followed heated at $250^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for about 4 min, and then cooled to room temperature. CsBr was repeatedly deposited seven times on Cs-Pb-Br precursor film to form the high-quality $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ film. Finally, the carbon paste was coated on the $\mathsf{F T O}/c\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}/m\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}/$ $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ film by a doctor-blade method, and dried at $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $15\mathrm{\min}$ to form the carbon back electrode. The active area was controlled to $0.09\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . All the above steps are performed in air atmosphere.
Measurements and Characterizations: The photovoltaic performances of PSCs were evaluated by recording photocurrent-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves under AM $\lvert1.5\textup{C}$ simulated solar illumination $(700m)\times100m^{-2}$ which was calibrated by a standard silicon device) at a scan rate of $0.1\ \vee\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . Ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra of $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ films were characterized using a Meipuda UV-3200 spectrophotometer. The surface morphology of perovskite film and the cross-sectional SEM image of PSCs were obtained by a field-emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, SU8220, Hitachi). The KPFM images and surface potentials of various perovskite films were performed using Kelvin probe force microscopy (AFM, Asylum Co. MFP-3D-SADV-OQ). The surface and cross-sectional PL mapping images were tested by the confocal fluorescence microscope (ISS Q2 FLIM/FFS, ISS Inc.). The crystal phases of ${\mathsf{C s P b B r}}_{3}$ films were characterized by X-ray diffractometer (PHILIPSPW1800 diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u-K}\alpha$ radiation). XPS spectra were obtained on an RBD upgraded PHI-5000C ESCA system (Perkin Elmer) with $\mathsf{M g}\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation $(h\nu=7253.6~\mathrm{eV}_{,}$ ). The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra and the TRPL decay spectra of perovskite films deposited on the glass substrates were recorded on a FluoroMax-4 spectrofluorometer excited by $385\ n m$ laser and a Horiba spectrometer excited by $375\ \mathsf{n m}$ laser, respectively. The EQE spectra were obtained with IPCE kit developed by Enli Technology Co., Ltd. The defect state densities of $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ films and hole mobilities for the hole-only devices with the structure of ${\mathsf{F T O}}/$ $\mathsf{N i O}_{x}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}/$ carbon were obtained with a SCLC model using the Mott-Gurney law recorded from 1 to $7\:\forall$ under dark condition. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra were recorded by the commercial FTIR system (Cary 680, Agilent). Ultraviolet photoemission spectroscopy (UPS) of various samples were performed by a Kratos AXIS ULTRA system using the concentric hemisphere analyzer. EIS of different PSCs were performed from 1  MHz to $0.1~\mathsf{H z}$ under the dark condition. The photostability was recorded under continuous illumination at the maximum power point (MPP) bias voltage $(V_{\mathsf{M P P}})$ , then the steady-state PCE was calculated with the formula: $\mathsf{P C E}_{\mathsf{M P P}}=$ $(\boldsymbol{J}_{\mathrm{MPP}}\times\boldsymbol{V}_{\mathrm{MPP}}/700)\times100\%$ .
Statistical Analysis: All statistical analyses were performed with Origin 2020b. The data obtained from DFT, SEM, AFM, KPFM, PL mapping, UV-vis, PL, TRPL, XPS, UPS, FTIR, XRD, $J{-}V,$ EQE, and contact angles were the original data without normalization. Linear fittings were applied to the dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ on the light intensity, and Mott– Schottky plots. Bi-exponential decay function was employed to TRPL decays to explore the carrier transport dynamics. 20 PSCs were used to generate the device performance statistics. The summarized champion PCEs for ${\mathsf{C s P b B r}}_{3}$ PCEs were gained from reported works.
DFT Calculation Details: The first principle calculations were performed by the Vienna ab initio simulation package.[57,58] The above program has the projected enhancement wave pseudopotential and the generalized gradient approximation of Perdew, Burke, and Enzzerhof (PBE), exchange-correlation functional, which is employed to optimize the structure and further obtain the free energy of all structures.[59,60] The cutoff energy of the plane waves basis set is $500~\mathrm{eV}$ and the Monkhorst-Pack mesh of $3\times3\times1$ was used in K-sampling in the adsorption energy calculation. Moreover, van der Waals interactions corrected using DFT-D2 were considered.[61] The electronic selfconsistent iteration is set to $10^{-5}~\mathrm{eV},$ and the positions of all the atoms are fully relaxed until the residual force on each atom is below $0.02\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ . 15 Å of vacuum layer along the $z$ -direction was employed to avoid periodic interactions. The free energy of structure A (GA) was calculated via the formula $\mathsf{C A}=\mathsf{E A}+\mathsf{Z P E}-\mathsf{T S}$ : the total energy (EA), zero-point energy (ZPE), $\tau$ is the temperature (298.15K), and the entropy (S) of structure A.
